    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Historians consider Manchukuo a puppet state: Japanese vice-ministers made every decision, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a> commander was also ambassador with a veto over the emperor, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a> was a figurehead.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a> used more than ten million Chinese civilians as slave labour, Unit 731 vivisected prisoners near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbin">Harbin</a>, and Special Security Courts sentenced thousands to death for 'thought crimes'.</li><li>Only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s allies and dependents recognised the state; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> endorsed the Lytton Report against it in February 1933 and Japan left the League.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Tokyo tribunal found that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> used the 'alleged but false independence of Manchukuo' to run a worldwide drug traffic; in 1937 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> was told that 90 per cent of the world's illicit white drugs were of Japanese origin.</p></aside>
    </section>

